WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.334
Begin by framing Foundations of Computation as
a question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:09.609
observable results.

3
00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:17.316
This course provides an introduction to the
theory of computation, including formal

4
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:21.812
languages, grammars, auto-mata theory,
computability, and complexity.

5
00:00:21.812 --> 00:00:29.263
Consider this worked case: A finite automaton
accepts the string 'abab' by transitioning

6
00:00:29.263 --> 00:00:34.421
through states that track alternating 'a' and
'b' characters.

7
00:00:34.421 --> 00:00:41.392
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:41.392 --> 00:00:41.890
conclusion.

9
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:47.703
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:54.970
Now define Formal Languages and Grammars
precisely enough that another learner could

11
00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.760
test the definition.

12
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:04.175
Automata and formal languages describe
machines, accepted strings, and computational

13
00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:04.916
limits.

14
00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:12.347
Consider this worked case: A context-free
grammar defines valid arithmetic expressions

15
00:01:12.347 --> 00:01:16.400
by recursively combining terms and operators.

16
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.680
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.963
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:24.963 --> 00:01:31.353
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:31.353 --> 00:01:38.265
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

20
00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:40.338
throughout the analysis.

21
00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:47.905
With the foundation in place, examine how
Automata Theory Basics operates in a concrete

22
00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:48.438
sequence.

23
00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:54.798
Asymptotic notation describes how resource use
grows with input size.

24
00:01:54.798 --> 00:02:01.321
Consider this worked case: A pushdown
automaton recognizes nested parentheses by

25
00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:07.251
pushing symbols onto a stack and popping them
during matching.

26
00:02:07.251 --> 00:02:14.251
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

27
00:02:14.251 --> 00:02:19.970
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

28
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:27.025
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

29
00:02:27.025 --> 00:02:29.141
throughout the analysis.

30
00:02:29.258 --> 00:02:36.032
Next, compare the choices involved in
Computability Limits and make the decision

31
00:02:36.032 --> 00:02:37.161
criteria explicit.

32
00:02:37.161 --> 00:02:44.942
Divide-and-conquer algorithms split a problem,
solve subproblems, and combine their results.

33
00:02:44.942 --> 00:02:52.119
Consider this worked case: The halting problem
demonstrates that no general algorithm can

34
00:02:52.119 --> 00:02:56.536
decide whether an arbitrary program will
eventually stop.

35
00:02:56.536 --> 00:03:03.446
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

36
00:03:03.446 --> 00:03:04.598
the goalposts.

37
00:03:04.598 --> 00:03:11.582
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

38
00:03:11.582 --> 00:03:18.397
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

39
00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.442
throughout the analysis.

40
00:03:20.546 --> 00:03:28.829
Apply Complexity and Resource Bounds to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

41
00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:29.933
isolated definition.

42
00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:34.402
Recurrences describe the cost of recursive
algorithms.

43
00:03:34.402 --> 00:03:43.850
Consider this worked case: A sorting algorithm
with O(n log n) time complexity processes

44
00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:48.574
larger inputs more efficiently than O(n^2)
alternatives.

45
00:03:48.574 --> 00:03:56.176
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

46
00:03:56.176 --> 00:03:56.683
step.

47
00:03:56.683 --> 00:04:01.808
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

48
00:04:01.922 --> 00:04:09.219
Examine the tradeoffs in Loop Invariants and
Proofs, including what improves and what

49
00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:10.903
becomes more difficult.

50
00:04:10.903 --> 00:04:17.622
A loop invariant supports a proof by
initialization, maintenance, and termination.

51
00:04:17.622 --> 00:04:25.383
Consider this worked case: In a binary search,
the invariant states that the target value, if

52
00:04:25.383 --> 00:04:28.778
present, lies within the current search
interval.

53
00:04:28.778 --> 00:04:35.434
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:42.528
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:48.724
Stress-test the reasoning around
Divide-and-Conquer Strategy with an edge case

56
00:04:48.724 --> 00:04:52.975
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

57
00:04:52.975 --> 00:04:59.365
Dynamic programming records solutions to
overlapping subproblems.

58
00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:06.890
Consider this worked case: Merge sort splits
an array into halves, recursively sorts each

59
00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:12.803
half, and merges the sorted halves into a
complete sorted array.

60
00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:19.240
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

61
00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:26.021
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

62
00:05:26.138 --> 00:05:33.679
Use Recurrence Relations to diagnose a failure
by separating symptoms, causes, and corrective

63
00:05:33.679 --> 00:05:34.259
action.

64
00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:41.773
Algorithm analysis connects a precise problem
specification to a correctness argument and

65
00:05:41.773 --> 00:05:43.025
resource bounds.

66
00:05:43.025 --> 00:05:54.897
Consider this worked case: The recurrence T(n)
= 2T(n/2) + O(n) describes the time complexity

67
00:05:54.897 --> 00:06:01.228
of merge sort, solving to O(n log n).

68
00:06:01.228 --> 00:06:09.116
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:06:09.116 --> 00:06:10.806
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:06:10.806 --> 00:06:18.228
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:06:18.338 --> 00:06:25.566
Connect Graph Algorithms and Reachability to
the earlier ideas and explain how the pieces

72
00:06:25.566 --> 00:06:27.631
work as one system.

73
00:06:27.631 --> 00:06:35.125
Graph algorithms operate on vertices and edges
to answer reachability, ordering,

74
00:06:35.125 --> 00:06:37.850
connectivity, and path questions.

75
00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:43.663
Consider this worked case: Breadth-first
search finds the shortest path in an

76
00:06:43.663 --> 00:06:47.538
unweighted graph by exploring neighbors level
by level.

77
00:06:47.538 --> 00:06:56.953
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

78
00:06:56.953 --> 00:06:58.522
across representations.

79
00:06:58.522 --> 00:07:05.944
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

80
00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:12.813
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

81
00:07:12.813 --> 00:07:13.296
analysis.

82
00:07:13.296 --> 00:07:20.684
A disciplined solution states inputs and
outputs, proves correctness, and then analyzes

83
00:07:20.684 --> 00:07:22.531
time and space.

84
00:07:22.531 --> 00:07:29.077
Consider this worked case: Before implementing
a sorting routine, verify the preconditions,

85
00:07:29.077 --> 00:07:35.078
prove the loop invariant, and derive the
recurrence for time bounds.

86
00:07:35.078 --> 00:07:43.054
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

87
00:07:43.054 --> 00:07:44.281
verification step.

88
00:07:44.281 --> 00:07:51.092
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

89
00:07:51.092 --> 00:07:51.578
intuition.
